Sibling `@media` rules for one property family are rewritten so the last
one written wins. An earlier rule is negated only against a later rule
that sets every longhand the earlier one sets. A later rule that sets
other longhands, or only some of them, leaves the earlier rule as written,
so its other longhands still apply where both conditions match.

`sides`: margin-top then margin-bottom - disjoint longhands, no rewrite.
`color_then_width`: border-top-color then border-width - disjoint, no
rewrite. `shorthand_then_side`: margin then margin-top - the later rule
covers one of four sides, no rewrite. `side_then_shorthand`: padding-top
then padding - the later rule covers it, so the earlier rule is negated.

A partial overlap is left to the sheet order. Inside one block, the block
order puts `margin: 1px` before `margin-top: 2px`. Across blocks,
`side_first` defines `margin-left` before `shorthand_second` defines
`margin`, and `CSS.merge(shorthand_second, side_first)` still puts
`margin: 3px` first, so `margin-left: 2px` wins at 900px and wider.

